\label{comp}
We note for further reference that
given o.s.  $E\subset A$, $F\subset B$. $G\subset C$ 
and maps $u\in MB(E, F)$ and $v\in MB(F, G)$, we have
$vu\in MB(E, G)$ and 
\begin{equation}\label{e21}\|vu\|_{mb} \le \|v\|_{mb}\|u\|_{mb}.\end{equation}

Consider o.s. $E_j\subset A_j$, $F_j\subset B_j$ ($j=1,2$).
    For any pair of  m.b. maps $u_j: E_j \to F_j$, the map $u_1\otimes u_2$
    extends to a map in $MB(E_1\otimes_{\max} E_2, F_1\otimes_{\max} F_2)$ such that
     \begin{equation}  \label{e10''}
     \|u_1\otimes u_2\|_{mb} \le \|u_1 \|_{mb}\| u_2\|_{mb}.
      \end{equation}

\label{kir}
Kirchberg   (see \cite[p. 189]{154}) proved that $ A$ has the WEP if and only if
the min and max norms coincide on $A\otimes \mathscr{C}$. In particular this happens whenever $A$ is injective.

\label{rcoh1}
The inclusion $M \to L_2(\tau)_{rc}$ is  completely contractive. Moreover,
for any $H$ the identity on $H$ defines a completely contractive map $H_{rc} \to H_{oh}$. In particular,
 the identity on $L_2(\tau)$ defines a completely contractive map $L_2(\tau)_{rc} \to L_2(\tau)_{oh}$.
